' A real, ordinary ArcoBASIC program meant to be compiled BY the Fission
' Compiler Substrate's native x86-64 backend (fission/amir/lower_x86_64.abas)
' -- see fission/tests/native_programs/hello_native.abas's own header
' comment for the directory convention. Exercises the FOURTH real batch of
' host-function-bridge coverage: real Random.* (fission/native_runtime/
' host_bridge.c's own "Batch 4" section), a real, byte-for-byte port of
' the oracle's own PCG32 generator (include/arco/random.hpp/
' src/runtime/random.cpp), confirmed identical via a direct unit-test
' harness comparing known seed/sequence outputs against the oracle's own
' class directly -- plus Time.Timestamp and Sleep.
'
' Random.Choice/Sample/Shuffle are exercised on BOTH a Number array and a
' String array, proving the real special-case, kind-preserving dispatch
' (see fission/amir/lower_x86_64.abas's own comment: these can't be
' uniform host-function-table entries at all, since their own real result
' kind depends on the INPUT array's own element kind, not a single fixed
' ABI signature) -- Random.Sample/Shuffle both return a real NEW array,
' never mutating the caller's own array in place (confirmed against the
' oracle, checked here too: `a` PRINTs unchanged after `Random.Shuffle(a,
' ...)`).
'
' Real, disclosed scope: `Random.Integer`'s own upper bound is kept well
' under 2^31 here deliberately -- this batch's own verification work
' surfaced a real, PRE-EXISTING bug in this native backend's own operand-
' loading path (`Fission_X86_64OperandLoadLine`/the assembler's own `mov
' $imm, reg` encoding), unrelated to Random.* itself: any integer literal
' outside the signed 32-bit range is silently corrupted (a real x86-64 ISA
' constraint -- `MOV r/m64, imm32` sign-extends -- this backend's own
' assembler never falls back to a real 64-bit-immediate encoding for it).
' Recorded in .agents/FISSION_SUBSTRATE_PROGRESS.md as a real, disclosed,
' NOT-yet-fixed gap (out of this batch's own host-function-bridge scope);
' this fixture works around it by staying within the range every real
' Random.Integer call in ordinary ArcoBASIC code already uses in practice.
' `Sleep`'s own return value is deliberately not PRINTed (see
' `arco_host_sleep`'s own comment: it returns Bool TRUE, not the oracle's
' own real NULL -- this whole native backend has no NULL/void value
' representation yet, a real, disclosed, separate, pre-existing gap) --
' called here purely as a statement, its own real, common use in practice.
'
' Expected output verified against legacy `ArcoFission compile-run` on this
' identical source as the equivalence-testing oracle.
h = Random.Create(100)
PRINT Random.Integer(0, 9, h)
PRINT Random.Integer(0, 9, h)
PRINT Random.Integer(0, 9, h)

a = [5, 10, 20, 30, 40, 50]
h2 = Random.Create(100)
PRINT Random.Choice(a, h2)

sa = ["red", "green", "blue", "yellow"]
h3 = Random.Create(100)
PRINT Random.Choice(sa, h3)

h4 = Random.Create(100)
shuffled = Random.Shuffle(a, h4)
PRINT shuffled
PRINT a

nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]
h5 = Random.Create(100)
sampled = Random.Sample(nums, 3, h5)
PRINT sampled

h6 = Random.Create(1)
ok = Random.Reseed(h6, 999)
PRINT ok
PRINT Random.Integer(0, 999999999, h6)

t = Time.Timestamp()
PRINT t > 1700000000

Sleep(1)
PRINT "slept"
